, {"tree": [30, 19, 11, 4.2]}, {"tree": [30, -24, 11, 4.2]}, {"tree": [-30, -29, 11, 4.2]}, {"tree": [-14, -29, 11, 4.2]}], "notes": "Gunnar Birkerts' building (1972) at the corner of Montrose Boulevard and Bissonnet Street: one parallelogram in plan, clad all over in ribbed stainless steel, its sharp acute corner pointing south-east at the street corner (deflecting Mies's Brown Pavilion across the way), the entrance a glazed notch cut into that corner, flat roof, no windows on the long faces. Refined 6 Oct 2026 from published descriptions (SAH Archipedia, the museum) and the ground file's OpenStreetMap outline, not photographs: the building was standing 40 m east of its outline (on 10%); it now sits on it, north-west of the junction the ground shows (Montrose the north-south street at x 0..14, Bissonnet at y 7..14), with the long faces along Bissonnet and a sharper angle (about 55 degrees) so the point reads; ribs every 1.25 m; streets and sidewalks redrawn where the ground has them, the side street to the north. Still guessed: the exact angle and length of the sides (the outline is in 7 m cells), the height (11 m), the rib spacing, the entrance's exact form, the planting."}